From 52d695fe506c49f236a3c3f40935308f941b69ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josh McKinney <joshka@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 01:04:00 +0300
Subject: git: keep fetched ref targets visible

Fetched Git refs are durable labels for the commit imported from the
remote ref. They are intentionally different from local bookmarks and
working-copy references: if a user edits or otherwise rewrites the fetched
change, the fetched ref should continue to name the original remote commit
rather than follow the local rewrite.

That distinction exposed a subtle visibility bug. Rewriting the fetched
commit records the old commit in parent_mapping, and update_heads() removes
those old commits from the visible heads. Normal jj references either move
as part of the rewrite machinery or keep the commit referenced elsewhere,
but fetched Git refs do not follow the rewrite. The view could therefore
still contain refs/pull/123/head@origin -> <old commit> while the old commit
was no longer visible. Commands like git ref list would report the durable
fetched ref, while log/revsets would resolve it only as a hidden commit.
Undoing a later refetch made this especially confusing because the fetched
ref entry was restored correctly, but its target was no longer connected to
the visible graph.

Preserve present fetched-ref targets as visibility heads during head
maintenance, and treat them as references when deciding whether to abandon a
working-copy commit. This keeps the remote-imported commit visible without
making fetched refs follow local rewrites.

Add a regression covering the dogfood case: fetch a pull-request ref, edit
and locally rewrite that fetched commit, fetch the same ref again, undo the
second fetch, and verify that the fetched ref still resolves to the original
non-hidden commit.

